      "data": {
        "grounded": true,
        "answer": "A commit in Git is a snapshot of the current state of the files in the repository. It has a unique ID and is linked to its parent, allowing you to travel back in time to a previous version of the files. [1] When you create a commit, you're taking a snapshot of the current state of the files, and the commit has a unique ID. [1] \n\nThe course doesn't cover what happens to the changed files after a commit is created.",
